ewtheorem{example}[theorem]{Example} \maketitle Let $ oxplus_n$ and $\Phi_n(\cdot)$ be defined as in the problem statement. In this note we prove the following result, which was conjectured by D. Shlyakhtenko.  egin{theorem} \label{thm:stamineq} Let $p(x)$ and $q(x)$ be any two monic real-rooted polynomials of degree $n$. Then $$ rac{1}{\Phi_n(p oxplus_n q)} \geq  rac{1}{\Phi_n(p)} +  rac{1}{\Phi_n(q)}.$$ \end{theorem} \section{Notation and preliminaries} \subsection{Polynomials and the finite free convolution} Given a polynomial $p(x)$ of degree $n$ we say that $\alpha=(\alpha_1, \dots, \alpha_n)$ is a vector of roots for $p(x)$ if the $\alpha_i$ are the roots of $p(x)$. We will say that $\alpha$ is ordered if $\alpha_1\geq \cdots \geq \alpha_n$. Recall that for monic polynomials $p(x)$ and $q(x)$, $p(x) oxplus_n q(x)$ may be expressed as:  egin{equation}\label{eqn:convroots} p(x) oxplus_n q(x) = \sum_{\pi\in S_n} \prod_{i=1}^n(x-\alpha_i- eta_{\pi(i)}),\end{equation} where $\alpha$ and $ eta$ are vectors of roots for $p(x)$ and $q(x)$, respectively, and $S_n$ is the symmetric group on $n$ elements (see Theorem 2.11 of \cite{marcus2022finite} for a proof). Walsh \cite{walsh1922location} proved that if $p(x)$ and $q(x)$ are real-rooted, then so is $p(x) oxplus_n q(x)$. Therefore, the finite free convolution induces a map $$oots:  R^{n}	imes  R^n 	o  R^n,$$ where if $\alpha$ and $ eta$ are vectors of roots for $p(x)$ and $q(x)$, then $oots(\alpha,  eta)$ is defined to be the ordered vector of roots for $p(x) oxplus_n q(x)$. Other than the fact that $ oxplus_n$ preserves real-rootedness, our proof will crucially exploit each of the following well-known properties of the finite free convolution. In what follows we will use $ one_n$ to denote the all-ones vector of dimension $n$. We will use the notation $$m_k(\alpha):=  rac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n \alpha_i^k \quad 	ext{and} \quad \var(\alpha) := m_2(\alpha)-m_1(\alpha)^2.$$  egin{proposition}[Properties of $ oxplus_n$] \label{thm:ffconvolution} If $\alpha,  eta\in  R^n$ and $\gamma =oots( \alpha,  eta)$, then:  egin{enumerate}[i)] \item \label{item:additivity} (Additivity) $m_1(\gamma)=m_1(\alpha)+m_1( eta)$ and $\var(\gamma) = \var(\alpha)+\var( eta)$. \item \label{item:translations} (Commutation with translation) For all $t\in  R$, $oots(\alpha+t one_n , eta) = \gamma +t one_n$ and $oots(\alpha ,  eta + t  one_n) = \gamma+t one_n$. \end{enumerate} \end{proposition}  egin{proof} (i) Follows from the definition of $p oxplus_n q$ in terms of the coefficients of $p$ and $q$ and the Newton identities. (ii) Follows from \eqref{eqn:convroots}. \end{proof} \subsection{The heat flow and the finite free Fisher information} Given a vector of roots $\alpha\in  R^n$ we will define the its finite free score vector $\score(\alpha) \in ( R\cup\{\infty\})^n$ as $$\score(\alpha) := \left( \sum_{j : j 
eq i}  rac{1}{\alpha_i-\alpha_j} ight)_{i=1}^n. $$ Given a real-rooted polynomial $p(x)$ with vector of roots $\alpha$, define its finite free Fisher information as $$\Phi_n(p) := \|\score(\alpha)\|^2.$$ The following fact will allow us to write the finite free Fisher information of the polynomial $p(x)$ in terms of the dynamics of its roots under the reverse heat flow. It was shown to us by D. Shlyakhtenko.  egin{lemma}[Score vectors as derivatives] \label{lem:score} Assume $p(x)$ has simple roots. Let $p_t(x):= \exp\left( - rac{t}{2} \partial_x^2 ight)p(x)$ and let $\alpha(t) = (\alpha_1(t), \dots, \alpha_n(t))$ be the ordered vector of roots of $p_t(x)$. Then $$\alpha_i'(0) = \sum_{j : j
eq i}  rac{1}{\alpha_i-\alpha_j},$$ and in particular $\alpha'(0) = \score(\alpha)$. \end{lemma}  egin{proof} Since the $\alpha_i(t)$ are continuous in $t$, the roots remain simple in a neighborhood of $t=0$. Implicitly differentiating the expression $$p(\alpha_i(t))-tp''(\alpha_i(t))/2+t^2R(\alpha_i(t),t)=0$$ (where $R(x,t)$ is a polynomial) at $t=0$ one obtains $$ \alpha_i'(0)= rac{1}{2} rac{p''(\alpha_i)}{p'(\alpha_i)},$$ which is equal to the advertised expression. \end{proof} \section{Proof of Stam's inequality} We now prove Theorem ef{thm:stamineq}. The following Lemma allows us to restrict attention to the case when $p,q$, and $p oxplus_n q$ all have simple roots.  egin{lemma}[Approximation by Simple Rooted Polynomials] Let $\epsilon>0$ and define the differential operator $T_\epsilon:=(1-\epsilon \cdot d/dx)^n$. If $p(x)$ is a monic real-rooted polynomial of degree $n$, then  egin{enumerate}[i)] \item $(T_\epsilon p)(x)$ is monic and real-rooted of degree $n$ with simple roots. \item $ \Phi_n(T_\epsilon p)ightarrow \Phi_n(p)$ as $\epsilonightarrow 0$. \item $(T_\epsilon p) oxplus_n (T_\epsilon q)= T_\epsilon^2 (p oxplus_n q)$. \end{enumerate} \end{lemma}  egin{proof} (i) was shown in \cite{nuij1968note}. (ii) is because $\Phi_n$ is continuous in the roots of $p$, which are continuous in $\epsilon$. (iii) follows because $ oxplus_n$ commutes with differential operators (see e.g. \cite{marcus2022finite}.)\end{proof} Thus, establishing Theorem ef{thm:stamineq} for the simple case implies the general case by using (iii) above and taking $\epsilonightarrow 0$. In what follows, $p(x)$ and $q(x)$ are monic real-rooted polynomials, $\alpha$ and $ eta$ are vectors of roots for $p(x)$ and $q(x)$, $\gamma:= oots(\alpha,  eta)$, and $\alpha, eta,\gamma$ all have distinct entries, implying that they are smooth functions of the coefficients of the corresponding polynomials. Let $\jac$ denote the Jacobian of $oots$ at the point $(\alpha,  eta)$. Our proof can be separated into three steps. The second step is the most substantial one and we will defer its detailed discussion to Section ef{sec:understandingjacob}. 	extbf{Step 1 (Jacobians and score vectors).} We first note that the following relation between score vectors holds.  egin{observation}[Relating score vectors] \label{obs:scorevecs} Using the above notation, for any $a, b \geq 0$ $$\jac (a\score(\alpha), b \score( eta)) = (a+b)\score(\gamma). $$ \end{observation}  egin{proof} For every $t\geq 0$ let $p_t(x) = \exp(-	frac{t}{2} \partial_x^2) p(x)$, let $\alpha(t)$ be the ordered vector of roots of $p_t$, and define $q_t, r_t$ and $ eta(t), \gamma(t)$ in an analogous way. Since the finite free convolution commutes with any differential operator, it follows that $$r_{(a+b)t} = p_{at}  oxplus_n q_{bt}. $$ Hence $\gamma((a+b)t) = oots(\alpha_{at},  eta_{bt})$ for every $t$. So, if we differentiate this relation with respect to $t$, using the chain rule for the right-hand side, we get $$(a+b)\gamma'(0) = \jac \left( egin{array}{c} a \cdot \alpha'(0) \ b \cdot eta'(0) \end{array} ight).$$ A direct application of Lemma ef{lem:score} concludes the proof. \end{proof} 	extbf{Step 2 (Understanding the Jacobian).} The substance of our proof lies in understanding $\jac$. In particular, we will show the following.  egin{proposition} \label{prop:singularvalues} If $u, v \in  R^n$ are orthogonal to $ one_n$ then $$\|\jac (u, v)\|^2 \leq \|u\|^2 + \|v\|^2.$$ \end{proposition} This proposition will be proven in Section ef{sec:understandingjacob}, for now we show how it is used. 	extbf{Step 3 (Proof of Theorem ef{thm:stamineq} \`a la Blachman).} With Observation ef{obs:scorevecs} and Proposition ef{prop:singularvalues} in hand we can conclude the proof using the same argument that Blachman used in \cite{blachman2003convolution}.  egin{proof}[Proof of Theorem ef{thm:stamineq}] First note that $$\sum_{i=1}^n \sum_{j : j
eq i}  rac{1}{\alpha_i-\alpha_j}=0,$$ since each term in the sum appears once with a plus and once with a minus. Therefore $\score(\alpha)$ is orthogonal to $ one_n$ and, arguing analogously, $\score( eta)$ is orthogonal to $ one_n$. So, Proposition ef{prop:singularvalues} implies $$\|\jac(a\score(\alpha), b \score( eta))\|^2 \leq a^2\|\score(\alpha)\|^2+b^2 \|\score( eta)\|^2.$$ Combining this with Observation ef{obs:scorevecs} yields $$(a+b)^2 \|\score(\gamma)\|^2 \leq a^2\|\score(\alpha)\|^2+ b^2\|\score( eta)\|^2. $$ Now, by choosing $a=  rac{1}{\|\score(\alpha)\|^2}$ and $b =  rac{1}{\|\score( eta)\|^2}$, the above inequality turns into $$\left( rac{1}{\|\score(\alpha)\|^2} +  rac{1}{\|\score( eta)\|^2} ight)^2 \|\score(\gamma)\|^2 \leq  rac{1}{\|\score(\alpha)\|^2} +  rac{1}{\|\score( eta)\|^2}, $$ which after simple algebraic manipulations can be turned into the inequality claimed in Theorem ef{thm:stamineq}. \end{proof} \subsection{Understanding $\jac$} \label{sec:understandingjacob} Let $(\Omega_{ oxplus_n, 1}, \dots, \Omega_{ oxplus_n, n})$ be the coordinate functions of $oots$, that is $\gamma_i = ootsi(\alpha,  eta)$. The starting point of our approach to proving Proposition ef{prop:singularvalues} is the observation that the matrix $\jac\jac^*$ is related to the Hessians of the functions $ootsi$. It will be helpful to introduce the notation $$\hessconv:= \mathrm{Hess}_{ootsi}.$$ For this discussion it will prove useful to define the $(2n-2)$-dimensional subspace $$\calV = \left\{ (u, v) \in  R^{n}	imes  R^n : u^*  one_n = v^* one_n=0 ight\}. $$ And, given $w\in  R^{n}	imes  R^n$ and $f:  R^{n}	imes  R^n	o  R^n$ we will use $D_wf$ to denote the directional derivative of $f$ in the direction of $w$, that is $D_w = \sum_i w_i\partial_i$.  egin{lemma}[The Hessian of $oots$] \label{lem:Hessian} Using the above notation  egin{equation} \label{eq:Hessian} w^* \jac \jac^* w = w^* \Big(I_{n}\oplus I_n- \sum_{i=1}^n \gamma_i \hessconv \Big)w,\quad  orall w\in \calV. \end{equation} \end{lemma}  egin{proof} Fix $w=(u, v)\in \calV$ and define $$\alpha(t):= \alpha+ tu, \quad  eta(t):= eta+tv, \quad 	ext{and}\quad \gamma(t):=oots(\alpha(t),  eta(t)),$$ and note that the variance additivity from Proposition ef{thm:ffconvolution} ef{item:additivity}) implies that $$m_2(\gamma(t))-m_1(\gamma(t))^2 = m_2(\alpha(t))+ m_2( eta(t))-(m_1(\alpha(t))^2+m_1( eta(t))^2). $$ Now, the fact that $(u,v)\in \calV$ implies that the means $m_1(\alpha(t))$ and $m_1( eta(t))$ are a constant function of $t$ and therefore, again by Proposition ef{thm:ffconvolution} ef{item:additivity}), the mean $m_1(\gamma(t))$ is also a constant function of $t$. So, differentiating the above equation twice with respect to $t$ we get  egin{equation} \label{eq:secderivatives} \left.\partial_t^2 m_2(\gamma(t))ight|_{t=0} = \left.\partial_t^2  ig(m_2(\alpha(t))+m_2( eta(t)) ig)ight|_{t=0} . \end{equation} Now we inspect both sides of the above equation. First  egin{align} 
onumber \left. n\, \partial_t^2m_2(\gamma(t))ight|_{t=0} & = \sum_{i=1}^n D_w^2(\gamma_i^2) \ 
onumber & = 2\sum_{i=1}^n \left( (D_w\gamma_i)^2+ \gamma_iD_w^2\gamma_iight) \ \label{eq:secondderofgamma} & = 2\left(w^* \jac \jac^* w+ \sum_{i=1}^n\gamma_i w^* \hessconv w ight). \end{align} Second  egin{align} 
onumber n\,\partial_t^2(m_2(\alpha(t))+m_2( eta(t))) & = \partial_t^2((\alpha+tu)^*(\alpha+tu)+( eta+tv)^*( eta+tv)) \ 
onumber & = 2(u^*u +v^*v) \ \label{eq:secondderofalphanadbeta} & = 2w^* w. \end{align} Finally, plugging (ef{eq:secondderofgamma}) and (ef{eq:secondderofalphanadbeta}) back into (ef{eq:secderivatives}) yields $$w^* \jac \jac^* w+ \sum_{i=1}^n\gamma_i w^* \hessconv w = w^* w,$$ which is equivalent to the advertised result. \end{proof} We now apply a result of Bauschke et al. \cite[Corollary 3.3]{bauschke2001hyperbolic}.  egin{theorem}[Bauschke et al.] \label{thm:convexity} Let $f\in  R[x_1, \dots, x_m]$ be a hyperbolic polynomial in the direction $w\in  R^m$ and for every $a\in  R^m$ let $\lambda_1(a)\geq \dots\geq \lambda_m(a)$ be the roots of $g_a(t):=f(a+tw)$. Then, for every $k=1, \dots, m$, the function $\sigma_k(a) := \sum_{i=1}^k \lambda_i(a)$ is convex in $a$. \end{theorem} In our context this implies the following.  egin{corollary} \label{cor:psdness} For any real numbers $c_1\geq \cdots \geq c_n $, the matrix $\sum_{i=1}^n c_i \hessconv$ is PSD. \end{corollary}  egin{proof} Define the multivariate polynomial $$f(x, a_1,\dots, a_n, b_1, \dots, b_n) := \sum_{\pi\in S_n} \prod_{i=1}^n (x-a_i-b_{\pi(i)}).$$ Since the above polynomial is homogeneous and the finite free convolution preserves real rootedness, $f$ is hyperbolic in the direction $e_1=(1, 0\cdots, 0)$. Now, by Theorem ef{thm:convexity} the functions $$\sigma_k(x, a, b) = \sum_{i=1}^k \lambda_i(x, a, b)$$ are convex, where $\lambda_1(x, a, b)\geq \cdots \geq \lambda_n(x, a, b)$ denote the roots of $f((x, a, b)+t e_1)$. And, because the $c_i$ are ordered we moreover have that the function $$L(x, a, b):=\sum_{i=1}^n c_i \lambda_i(x,a, b)$$ is convex, as it can be written as a positive linear combination of the $\sigma_k$. It follows that $\mathrm{Hess}_{L} = \sum_{i=1}^n c_i \mathrm{Hess}_{\lambda_i}$ at any $(x, a, b)$ is PSD. But, on the other hand, when $x=0$, $a=\alpha$ and $b =  eta$, we have that $\mathrm{Hess}_{\lambda_i} = \hessconv$, which in turn gives that $\sum_{i=1}^n c_i \hessconv$ is PSD. \end{proof} We can now complete the proof of Proposition ef{prop:singularvalues}.  egin{proof}[Proof of Proposition ef{prop:singularvalues}] Let $(u, v)\in \calV$. Then $$\|\jac(u, v)\|^2 = (u, v)^* \jac \jac^* (u, v) = \|u\|^2+\|v\|^2-\sum_{i=1}^n \gamma_i (u, v)^* \hessconv (u, v),$$ where the last equality follows from Lemma ef{lem:Hessian}. Now, applying Corollary ef{cor:psdness} with $c_i=\gamma_i$ gives that $\sum_{i=1}^n \gamma_i \hessconv$ is PSD, and hence $$\sum_{i=1}^n \gamma_i (u, v)^* \hessconv (u, v)\geq 0.$$ The proof follows from putting the two expressions together. \end{proof}  ibliographystyle{alpha}  ibliography{BibStam}
